Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(seo): failure-mode atlas, link-density gate, and a
 live price bug
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cycle 2. The guards from the previous commit, pointed at the whole tree, plus
the one content gap the topic map had recorded and left open.

THE LIVE BUG

The homepage said a typical 1,000 sq ft main floor runs about $4,500–$7,000,
four hundred pixels below a card stating $4.75–$7.50 per square foot. A thousand
square feet at the published band is $4,750–$7,500. A company whose entire pitch
is "fixed price in writing" was publishing two prices for one job on one screen
and understating the top of its own range by $500.

Three more hand-typed copies of the bands sat in the same component's tier
cards, under a stale warning telling the next reader to replace numbers that had
since been corrected. Every figure in that component is derived now.

The previous guard missed all four because it knew one shape of price. It did
not match `pricePerSqFt: '4.75–7.50'` (the $ and the unit are separate spans) or
`$4,500` (no cents). A guard that sees one of the shapes a price can take
reports the tree as clean and is worse than no guard. Three shapes now, and it
no longer fires on its own documentation — F-58/F-106 for the fourth time.

THE FAILURE-MODE ATLAS

/hardwood-floor-problems-toronto closes the only cluster marked coverage:'gap'.
Cupping, seasonal gapping, crowning, buckling, edge peeling — cause, honest
prognosis, and which published band the remedy lands in. Definitions come from
the glossary rather than being restated, and the page emits DefinedTerm nodes
pointing back at them. Two of the five say the correct action is to do nothing,
which is why the other three are believable.

LINK DENSITY (P2)

verify-links asks whether a page has a way in. This asks whether it has enough
ways out. On its first run FIVE of nine commercial and decision canonicals
reached ZERO case studies — a site with five published case studies carrying MVTR
readings, particle counts and 18-month deflection data, and not one page a buyer
lands on linked to any of them. /guides/[slug] reached zero services and its only
CTA sent people to score someone else's quote; /framework had no estimate CTA at
all. All fixed in content, with anchor text that says where it goes. The guard
also prints an `unres` column for hrefs it cannot statically resolve, so a zero
next to six unresolvable links reads as "the scanner is blind", not "the page is
broken".

ALSO

· Deletes 0001searchai20260824.patch and 0082truthcontrolprobe.patch from the
  repo root and gitignores *.patch. The first is this branch's own patch,
  committed to main by a web upload that stripped its dashes — which is why
  `git am` could not find it and the previous cycle silently never applied.
· /products/floorforge had no BreadcrumbList — the only deep page without one.
· Two FALSE findings in the previous audit, corrected: floorforge's canonical
  has always existed (it is in the sibling layout, which a 'use client' page
  requires), and three "missing H1" pages have theirs in a component or in MDX.
  The fix for any of those would have been to add a second H1.

VERIFICATION

tsc: 107 errors, identical set to baseline. Webpack: "Compiled successfully in
26.7s" — every module including the new pages. Full next build still blocked
here by Google Fonts and the Prisma binary host, both 403 from this sandbox. All
27 existing verify:* pass; all 7 seo gates pass.

